Idempotent Verbs
----------------
  --> PUT
  --> OPTIONS (also safe)
  --> HEAD (also safe)
  --> DELETE
       Idempotency in the context of HTTP refers to the intended effect on the server, not the response code returned to the client

           Same Server State:
                    The effect of a single DELETE request is that the resource is removed.
                    If you send the exact same DELETE request multiple times, the final state of the server remains the same: the resource is deleted (or remains deleted).

           Different Responses are Acceptable:
                  The first DELETE request might successfully return a 200 OK or 204 No Content,
                   while subsequent identical requests may return a 404 Not Found because the resource is already gone. The varying response
                    codes do not break the idempotency principle because the end result on the server is the same

   Safe:: meaning it is designed only to retrieve metadata (headers) about a resource without
   modifying the server state. PUT and DELETE changes the server state.


